PRIVATE REFLECTION: The position is after 34.Kg5 with Black to move. White Kg5, Qa5, Rd8, Nf4, pawn g3, Black Kg8, Rh2, Re8, Ne5, pawn g6.

The only checking first moves are Rh5+ on the h-file and Nf3+ with the e5-knight. Rh5+ is decisive. The rook on h2–h5 gives check along the 5th rank. h3/h4 are empty after Kg4–f5–g5.

King g5 escapes:
* Kxh5 is covered by g6-pawn;
* Kg6 is occupied by the g-pawn and covered by Ne5;
* Kh6, Kh4 are covered by the h-rook;
* Kf5 is covered by Rh5 and g6-pawn;
* Kg4 is covered by Ne5;
* Kf4 is occupied by White’s own Nf4;
only Kf6 remains.

After 1…Rh5+ Kf6 the mate is delivered on move 2. 
* 2…Rf5# is mate: Rf5 checks on the f-file, f5 is defended by g6-pawn, e7/e6 are covered by Re8, f7/g7 are covered by Kg8, g5 is covered by Rh5, e5 is now empty but covered by Re8, and the king has no other move. The rook move is Rf5, often written Rxf5#.
The same Kf6 can be mated by 2…Ng4#. Ng4+ checks from f6, is unblockable and the king has no flight; e7/e6 are covered by Re8, f7/g7 by Kg8, g5 by Rh5, f5 by Rh5/g6-pawn, e5 is vacated but covered by Re8. The Ng4# line is the equivalent knight finish noted by C2/C5.

The alternative king move Kf4 is legally impossible because f4 is occupied by White’s Nf4, which also removes the Kf4 defence claimed in the initial discussion. The consensus from round 1 is Rh5+ forces Kf6 and mates in two, with the main notation Rh5+ Kf6 Rf5# and the equivalent knight mate Rh5+ Kf6 Ng4#. The disputed notation Rxf5# vs Rf5# is a notational point; the move is a rook to f5 delivering mate. Unresolved is whether the preferred presentation uses the rook finish or the knight finish, both are mate in two.

