2.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: Position after 34.Kg5 is Black to move. The only forcing check is the 5th-rank h-file sacrifice 1...Rh2-h5+.

Escape analysis is settled:
* Kxh5 is illegal, ...g6 covers h5;
* Kxg6 is illegal, Ne5 covers g6;
* Kg4 is illegal, Ne5 covers g4;
* Kh4/Kh6 are on the h-file of the checking rook;
* Kf5 is covered by Rh5/…g6;
* Kf4 is illegal, White Nf4 occupies f4;
* remaining flights are covered by Kg8/Re8.

Hence the only legal White reply is 2.Kf6.

On f6 the king is shut in. The mating net is delivered on the f-file with the rook:
2...Rh5-f5#. f5 is empty, so the correct notation is Rf5 not Rxf5. Kxf5 is met by ...g6, e7/e6 are covered by Re8, f7/g7 are covered by Kg8, g5/e5 are covered by Rh5/Re8 and the g6-pawn, g6 is occupied, and Ne5 remains on e5 so Kxg6 remains illegal.

Ng4# is the same two-move idea in substance but is not a mate after 1...Rh5+ 2.Kf6 because the e5-knight vacates the defence of g6 on ...Ng4, leaving Kxg6 as a legal escape from f6. The rook finish Rf5# keeps Ne5 on e5 and retains the defence of g6.

Consensus now is the forced mate in two: Rh5+ Kf6 Rf5#. Earlier slips Rxf5# and the claim that Ng4# is mate after Kf6 are corrected; Kf4 defence was also previously mis-considered but is blocked by White Nf4.

